# Big Pickle — findings by Qwen 3.8 Max (SELF-EXCLUDED)

- Source: OpenCode Zen (`opencode/big-pickle`; stealth codename, provider undisclosed; **no AA page / no tracker coverage**)
- Date: 2026-11-01 (UTC)
- Overview and scoring methodology: `../../model-comparison.md`
- Cross-model signed log: `../../model-findings.md`

> **Verdict — SELF-EXCLUDED.** **Big Pickle** is **not a stable, identifiable model** — it is **OpenCode Zen's long-running anonymous "stealth" free slot** (200K / 32K output, text-only, **$0/$0/$0**). Scoring it is impossible under the research-permanence and no-invented-numbers rules because **(a) there is no fixed artifact to score** — the slot has been **rotated/swapped in place** (documented as "Code Supernova" 2025-10-02 → "Big Pickle" from 2025-11-18; the curated `idNote` states *"the underlying checkpoint has been swapped at least once in place, so no score can be attributed to a stable model"*), and forensic probing shows it **"may itself route"** to multiple backends; **(b) its identity is unresolved even by a dedicated peer study** — the vendor/maintainer once called it **GLM-4.6 (2025)**, the community now reads it as **DeepSeek-class**, and the black-box verification paper could only narrow it to a **"GLM-5 / GLM-5.1 candidate pool"** at **"low-to-medium confidence"** and explicitly declines to name it ("**ground truth may never arrive**… **cannot fully attribute**… not a forced guess at a checkpoint"); and **(c) there is no authoritative or independent benchmark** — **no Artificial Analysis page**, and the curated record cites **exactly one third-party number (SWE-Atlas 50.8%)** with "no tracker coverage." A capability score here would attribute shifting, unidentified backends to a fake fixed model — so the entry is excluded, not scored.

## Why this is a self-exclusion (not a scored model)

- **The slot has been swapped in place — there is no stable checkpoint.** **arXiv 2608.31142 §5.2 ("Long-running anonymous case: Big Pickle")** documents the OpenCode Zen catalog timeline: the stealth slot was occupied by **"Code Supernova" from 2025-10-02**, then **rotated to "Big Pickle" from 2025-11-18**, still anonymous 8+ months later at the 2026-08-28 audit. The curated `idNote` agrees verbatim: **"no score can be attributed to a stable model."** Any dimension score would be a snapshot of whichever backend happened to serve that day.
- **It may itself be a router, not one model.** The **instax-dutta forensic probe (Oct 2026, ~130 opencode-CLI probes)** found Big Pickle emitting **two distinct base templates** for identical fixed prompts (38.1K vs 44.4K token footprints) and concludes it **"behaves like a router itself and is not a clean control"** — i.e., multiple tokenizers/backends behind one card. A single capability vector cannot describe a multi-backend route.
- **Independent identity verification explicitly refuses to name it.** The **arXiv IRIS-style black-box protocol** reached only a **"most-consistent pool: GLM-5 / GLM-5.1 family by configuration exclusion"** with **confidence "low-to-medium"**; **Stages 2–3 (tokenizer fingerprinting / behavioral cross-checks) "were not executed because the Zen channel did not expose usable prompt_tokens"** (a data-availability bound). The paper's own framing is **"the operational meaning of *cannot fully attribute* under channel constraints, not a forced guess at a checkpoint."** The community view diverges (Reddit r/opencode *"Is Big Pickle DeepSeek now?"*; daniloradenovic lists it as an unrevealed stealth codename); the maintainer historically said GLM-4.6. **Three credible accounts name three different models** — the identity is genuinely unsettled.
- **No Artificial Analysis page / no tracker coverage** → **no Intelligence Index, no Speed, no Cost, no independent SWE-bench / GPQA / Terminal-Bench.** The **only** quantified measurement in any source is **one third-party SWE-Atlas 50.8%** (curated tradeoff: *"One third-party measurement … and no tracker coverage; treat its capability as unknown."*), tied to an unknown live checkpoint and an unreproducible harness.
- **Free + data-in-scope, not a product.** Zen lists it **$0/$0/$0, no published end date**, and warns that during the free window **collected data may be used to improve the model** — a preview pipeline, not a distributed product with a spec sheet or license.

## What we did establish (documented, not scored)

- **Program identity:** an **OpenCode Zen stealth-model slot** (same program as Space Bunny, Fledge Alpha, Ox Alpha, etc.), anonymous for **~11 months** by Oct 2026 — the **longest-unrevealed** case (prior stealth models revealed within days-to-weeks under their real names).
- **Declared surface spec:** **200,000 context / 32,000 max output**, **text in/out only**, reachable **only from within the OpenCode CLI** (direct API calls return `403 FreeTierError`).
- **Candidate underlying backends (unresolved):** **GLM-4.6** (maintainer-era, 2025) → **GLM-5 / GLM-5.1 pool** (arXiv, config-exclusion, low-med confidence) → **DeepSeek-class** (community feel-probe, mid-2026); possibly a **multi-backend router**. None confirmed; none uniquely attributable.

> **Consistency check:** unlike `owl-alpha` (a *verified* alias whose base — LongCat-2.0 — was already scored, so its data was simply *not re-credited*), Big Pickle has **no confirmed base to point at**, so there is nothing even to *decline* to double-count. It is closest to `grok-5` (no stable artifact) plus an extra **slot-rotation / router** instability. **No dimension scores are assigned.**

